  "narrative": "All estimates and intervals are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive evidence supports the claimed direction and no additional sign reversal is needed. Scientifically meaningful effects are absolute differences of at least 0.15 composite dependency-score units. More negative dependency_composite values mean stronger composite dependency.\n\nDue assessments. H104 tests MSI-high in TP53-, SMARCA4-, and KEAP1-intact samples. Discovery R96 gave signed estimate +0.745, 95% interval [0.666, 0.825]; independent validation R106 gave +0.790 with independent interval [0.683, 0.898]. Both point estimates and both lower bounds are far above 0.15, with adequate cells. This strongly supports the claim that MSI-high is associated with stronger composite dependency even in a background free of the three largest single-gene loss effects. It is a within-dataset observational association and not a causal or generalizable statement. Accept and close.\n\nH55 tests a formal interaction: KEAP1-loss effect on dependency within adenocarcinoma, compared with KEAP1-loss effect in TP53-intact complement. Discovery R42 was +0.274 [0.052, 0.496], but the lower bound was below the 0.15 threshold. Independent validation R107 was -0.134 [-0.563, 0.294], opposite in direction and wide enough to include zero and the threshold. The validation does not replicate the discovery interaction. I keep H55 rejected and closed: this specific interaction is not supported as a meaningful effect in this dataset. This is not proof that no interaction exists; it means the supplied evidence is inconsistent and underpowered for this claim.\n\nH134 tests PTEN loss in the bottom mutation-burden quartile. Discovery R122 was +0.207 [0.010, 0.405]; independent validation R133 was +0.350 with independent interval [0.087, 0.613]. The direction is consistent and the validation point estimate is substantively above threshold and excludes zero, but the stringent lower bound is below 0.15 because of the small PTEN-loss cell. I accept H134 cautiously as a supported within-dataset subgroup association, but I qualify it: the evidence strongly supports a stronger-dependency PTEN-loss association in low mutation burden, while high-confidence certainty that the true effect is entirely above the 0.15 threshold is limited. Close this stratum to avoid low-power duplicate tests.\n\nConsolidated supported findings. Five measured predictors or markers have scientifically meaningful associations with dependency_composite in this dataset. SMARCA4 loss is the strongest stronger-dependency predictor: overall signed contrast about +0.83 [0.77, 0.90] in discovery R4 and about +0.80 [0.71, 0.90] in validation R13. It remains robust in TP53- and KEAP1-intact backgrounds, co-occurrence contexts, adenocarcinoma, adult samples, non-Avana and KY libraries, screen batches, clean triple-intact samples, high and low mutation burden, high aneuploidy, high stemness, and PTEN-loss contexts. MSI-high is the second strongest stronger-dependency marker: signed discovery/validation contrasts about +0.79 to +0.81, validated overall in R88 and R93, and robust in adenocarcinoma, adult samples, high mutation burden, technical-batch contexts, and triple-intact genetic backgrounds including the new R106 response. KEAP1 loss is robustly associated with weaker dependency, with overall signed estimates about +0.83 and +0.80 in discovery/validation R6 and R26, and stable in clean, co-occurrence, batch, library, mutation-burden, and aneuploidy contexts. TP53 loss is also robustly associated with weaker dependency, overall about +0.42 and +0.38 in R1 and R39, with stable behavior in SMARCA4- and KEAP1-intact backgrounds, high mutation burden, high ploidy, adenocarcinoma, adult samples, and screen-batch contexts. PTEN loss is a smaller but scientifically meaningful stronger-dependency association, overall about +0.23 to +0.34 in R9 and R66, and now supported in clean triple-intact samples, TP53-intact samples, KEAP1-loss samples, adult samples, adenocarcinoma, batch_A, batch_B, and low mutation burden. The PTEN effect is consistently smaller than SMARCA4, MSI-high, KEAP1, or TP53 effects and remains the most context-sensitive of the accepted predictors.\n\nMajor negative findings. Many measured alterations were not supported or were in the opposite claimed direction: KRAS mutation, MTAP loss, ARID1A loss, STK11 loss, CDKN2A loss, BRCA2 loss, EGFR amplification, ERBB2 amplification, APC mutation, SMAD4 loss, NF1 loss, BRAF V600E, research markers D/E/F, MYC amplification overall, and MYC amplification conditional on high MYC RNA expression. Technical and environmental variables including media serum percentage, screen batch, sex after validation, matched RNA/DNA availability, RNA availability, and library-category contrasts were below threshold or incompatible with the claimed directions. Several originally registered opposite-direction claims are important negative findings: the original KEAP1 stronger-dependency direction, PTEN weaker-dependency direction, and MSI-high weaker-dependency direction were rejected. Formal interaction testing was mostly unsupported. The KEAP1 by TP53 interaction in adenocarcinoma failed validation here; many PTEN and core-gene interactions were rejected, near null, or remain unresolved. The data support robust main-effect and direct subgroup contrasts more than large, stable epistatic interaction claims.\n\nUnresolved uncertainty. Remaining scientific uncertainty is mainly in smaller PTEN context modification and formal interactions. PTEN in high mutation burden, some library and batch interaction contexts, PTEN by TP53-loss context, PTEN by age interaction, and several KEAP1 or PTEN formal interaction hypotheses remain underpowered or inconclusive. The proposed low-mutation-burden context for PTEN is now cautiously accepted but still threshold-uncertain at high confidence. Biological mechanism, causal interpretation, and external generalizability remain unresolved. Research signatures were not prioritized as direct predictors here and remain scientifically unaddressed in this run.\n\nNext analysis plan. The most informative next discovery analysis is PTEN loss within TP53-intact, low mutation-burden samples. This is the intersection of two accepted but smaller-effect contexts: PTEN loss in TP53-intact samples and PTEN loss in low mutation burden. If this intersection shows a contrast above 0.15, PTEN context stability will be strengthened; if attenuated, the PTEN association should be qualified as background-dependent rather than broadly stable. I also registered direct low mutation-burden tests of TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss to complete robustness profiling of the two stronger main effects across mutational burden. These are discovery contrasts only; they do not establish causality, mechanism, or generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Round 10 synthesis final report update. Due-deadline assessments: H104 accepted/closed after R106 validation, MSI-high remains robust in triple-intact background. H55 rejected/closed because discovery R42 was only marginal and validation R107 was opposite and wide. H134 accepted cautiously/closed after validation R133: PTEN low mutation burden is direction-supported and substantively positive, but stringent lower bound is below threshold. Supported within-dataset predictors: SMARCA4 loss and MSI-high stronger dependency; KEAP1 loss and TP53 loss weaker dependency; PTEN loss smaller stronger dependency with broad but context-sensitive support. Major negatives include KRAS, MTAP, ARID1A, STK11, CDKN2A, BRCA2, EGFR, ERBB2, APC, SMAD4, NF1, BRAF, constructed markers, MYC amp (including high MYC RNA), technical/library/environmental factors, sex, and most formal interactions. Unresolved: smaller PTEN context interactions, formal interaction hypotheses, and research signatures. Next registered discovery probes: PTEN in TP53-intact low TMB as most informative; TP53 in low TMB; KEAP1 in low TMB. No causal, mechanistic, or external-generalizability claims are accepted."
